{"name":"Paradise Sky","hex":"#66c6d0","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"paradise","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The place where sanctified souls are believed to live after death.","A very pleasant place, such as a place full of lush vegetation."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of Paradise (“Heaven”).","Alternative letter-case form of Paradise (“the Garden of Eden”)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/paradise"},{"term":"sky","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Sikaiana."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The atmosphere above a given point, especially as visible from the surface of the Earth as the place where the sun, moon, stars, and clouds are seen.","With a descriptive word: the part of the sky which can be seen from a specific place or at a specific time; its climate, condition, etc."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sky"}],"entries":[{"title":"Paradise Sky","blocks":[{"text":"Paradise Sky is a novel written by American author Joe R. Lansdale, published by Mulholland Books. It takes place in the post American Civil War era."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/8/8a/Paradise_Sky.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paradise_Sky","wikidata":"Q22682087"},{"title":"Paradise","blocks":[{"text":"In religion and folklore, paradise is a place of everlasting happiness, delight, and bliss. Paradisiacal notions are often laden with pastoral imagery, and may be cosmogonical, eschatological, or both, often contrasted with the miseries of human civilization: in paradise there is only peace, prosperity, and happiness. Paradise is a place of contentment, a land of luxury and fulfillment containing ever-lasting bliss and delight. Paradise is often described as a \"higher place\", the holiest place, in contrast to this world, or underworlds such as hell."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Angelico%2C_giudizio_universale_01.jpg/330px-Angelico%2C_giudizio_universale_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paradise","wikidata":"Q3363340"},{"title":"Sky","blocks":[{"text":"The sky is an unobstructed view upward from the surface of the Earth. It includes the atmosphere and outer space. It may also be considered a place between the ground and outer space, thus distinct from outer space."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/July_night_sky_%2835972569256%29.jpg/330px-July_night_sky_%2835972569256%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sky","wikidata":"Q527"},{"title":"Sky blue","blocks":[{"text":"Sky blue refers to a collection of shades comparable to that of a clear daytime sky. Typically it is a shade of cyan or light teal, though some iterations are closer to light azure or light blue. The term is attested from 1681. A 1585 translation of Nicolas de Nicolay's 1576 Les navigations, peregrinations et voyages faicts en la Turquie includes \"the tulbant [turban] of the merchant must be skie coloured\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Shades_of_light_blue.png/330px-Shades_of_light_blue.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sky_blue","wikidata":"Q373160"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Gardens_of_Ashurbanipal.jpg/500px-Gardens_of_Ashurbanipal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Gardens_of_Ashurbanipal.jpg/1280px-Gardens_of_Ashurbanipal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Gardens_of_Ashurbanipal.jpg/500px-Gardens_of_Ashurbanipal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The luxurious palace and gardens of Neo-Assyrian king Ashurbanipal (ruled 668–631 BCE) at Nineveh, with original color reconstitution. Irrigation canals radiate from an aqueduct. The king appears under the porch. British Museum.[8][9]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGardens_of_Ashurbanipal.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Jan_Bruegel_d._%C3%84._003.jpg/500px-Jan_Bruegel_d._%C3%84._003.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Jan_Bruegel_d._%C3%84._003.jpg/1280px-Jan_Bruegel_d._%C3%84._003.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Jan_Bruegel_d._%C3%84._003.jpg/500px-Jan_Bruegel_d._%C3%84._003.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Paradise by Jan Bruegel, circa 1620","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJan_Bruegel_d._%C3%84._003.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Nicolas_Poussin_-_Le_Printemps.jpg/500px-Nicolas_Poussin_-_Le_Printemps.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Nicolas_Poussin_-_Le_Printemps.jpg/1280px-Nicolas_Poussin_-_Le_Printemps.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Nicolas_Poussin_-_Le_Printemps.jpg/500px-Nicolas_Poussin_-_Le_Printemps.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Nicolas Poussin, Four seasons of paradise, 1660–1664","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANicolas_Poussin_-_Le_Printemps.jpg"},{"srcs